17. REQUIRED PRODUCT AND DATA INTEGRITY RULES

These rules take precedence over cosmetic completeness.

Pronunciation measurements
measured: an actual supported provider measurement.
estimated: a transparently labeled approximation with documented methodology.
unavailable: no defensible measurement could be produced.

Do not map unavailable metrics to zero.

Do not create artificial numerical confidence or improvement scores.

Keep metric-level availability when a provider supports some metrics but not others.

Baseline and final reports

Do not generate a valid before/after comparison without comparable source data.

Do not label a recording as day 90 if it was recorded at a different time.

Do not claim a learner improved because a training session was completed.

Audio and privacy

Separate the ability to process audio from permission to retain it.

Honor consent withdrawal and deletion requests.

Delete temporary files after processing.

Use private storage and authenticated access.

Content quality

Mark unreviewed phonetics content clearly in internal metadata.

Do not represent heuristic L1 confusion maps as established facts about every Tamil or Arabic speaker.

Support individual variation and allow a learner's actual measured difficulties to override general heuristics.

Cost controls

Add configurable limits for scoring attempts, AI requests, transcription, and audio storage.

Prefer cached reusable reference audio over repeated generation.

Prevent accidental retry loops from generating uncontrolled provider costs.

